body:

One of the most common mistakes when working with large datasets in PostgreSQL
is fetching everything into memory at once. It is the path of least
resistance: you write your query, call ``fetchall()``, and iterate over the
results. For small datasets this is fine. For large ones, you will quickly
find your application consuming far more memory than it needs to.

.. CUT-HERE

Even after closing the cursor, Python’s memory allocator might hold on the
memory used longer than expected; a long-running service might end up with a
large memory footprint in a server. So this is usually a condition you will
want to avoid.

Psycopg offers a cleaner solution: `server-side cursors`__, which allow you to
stream results from the database row by row, keeping your memory footprint
small and predictable regardless of how large the result set is. Server-side
cursors are available in both Psycopg 2 and Psycopg 3.

.. __: https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/advanced/cursors.html#server-side-cursors


How it works
------------

When you execute a query normally, PostgreSQL runs it, assembles the full
result set, and sends it to the client. With a `server-side cursor`__,
PostgreSQL instead keeps the result set on the server and sends rows to the
client in batches as they are requested. This means your Python process only
ever holds a small number of rows in memory at any one time. Giving the cursor
a name is what tells Psycopg to use a server-side cursor rather than a
client-side one.

.. __: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-declare.html

.. code:: python

    import psycopg

    with psycopg.connect("dbname=mydb") as conn:
        with conn.cursor(name="my_cursor") as cur:
            cur.execute("SELECT * FROM very_large_table")
            for row in cur:
                process(row)

Everything else looks identical to a normal cursor: you can iterate over it,
call `fetchone()`__, or call `fetchmany(n)`__ to retrieve a specific number of
rows at a time.

.. __: https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/api/cursors.html#psycopg.Cursor.fetchone
.. __: https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/api/cursors.html#psycopg.Cursor.fetchmany

In Psycopg 3, server-side cursors are exposed through the `ServerCursor
class`__, which may also be instantiated directly. In Psycopg 2 only the name
parameter can be used.

.. __: https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/api/cursors.html#psycopg.ServerCursor


Controlling batch size
----------------------

By default, Psycopg 3 fetches rows in batches of 100 when iterating over a
server-side cursor. You can control this with the `itersize attribute`__:

.. __: https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/api/cursors.html#psycopg.ServerCursor.itersize

.. code:: python

    with conn.cursor(name="my_cursor") as cur:
        cur.itersize = 2000
        cur.execute("SELECT * FROM very_large_table")
        for row in cur:
            process(row)

Larger batch sizes reduce the number of round-trips to the database but
increase memory usage per batch. The right value depends on the size of your
rows and your application's memory constraints. For most use cases the default
of 100 is a reasonable starting point.

``itersize`` only affects the iteration on the cursor using for. Other methods
such as ``fetchone()``, and ``fetchmany(n)`` will fetch exactly the number of
rows requested.


Using server-side cursors with async
------------------------------------

Psycopg 3's async support extends naturally to server-side cursors, making it
straightforward to stream large result sets in an async context without
blocking:

.. code:: python

    import psycopg

    async with await psycopg.AsyncConnection.connect("dbname=mydb") as conn:
        async with conn.cursor(name="my_cursor") as cur:
            await cur.execute("SELECT * FROM very_large_table")
            async for row in cur:
                await process(row)

The API is deliberately symmetric with the synchronous version. Just add the
await keyword where needed.


A practical example: exporting large datasets
---------------------------------------------

A common real-world use case is exporting data to a file. Without server-side
cursors, exporting a table with millions of rows means loading all of them
into memory before you can write a single line. With streaming, you can write
rows to disk as they arrive:

.. code:: python

    import csv
    import psycopg

    with psycopg.connect("dbname=mydb") as conn:
        with conn.cursor(name="export_cursor") as cur:
            cur.execute("SELECT id, name, email FROM users ORDER BY id")
            with open("users.csv", "w", newline="") as f:
                writer = csv.writer(f)
                writer.writerow(["id", "name", "email"])
                for row in cur:
                    writer.writerow(row)

This will happily export a table of any size while using only a few kilobytes
of memory for the current batch of rows.


Things to be aware of
---------------------

Server-side cursors require an open transaction for their lifetime. Psycopg 3
handles this automatically, but it is worth knowing that this transaction
exists and must be kept open on the server as long as the cursor is used. For
long-running exports or processing jobs this is usually fine, but be mindful
of holding transactions open unnecessarily in high-concurrency environments.


Summary
-------

Server-side cursors are one of those features that you may not need every day,
but when you do need them: processing exports, running aggregations over large
tables, streaming data to downstream systems, they make a significant
difference. The API is clean, the behaviour is predictable, and the memory
savings for large datasets are substantial.

If you find Psycopg useful in your work, please consider `sponsoring the
project <https://github.com/sponsors/psycopg/>`_ to help ensure its continued
development.
